{"kind":"promise","text":"Promised, on live bases and with no dead-basis override.\n\nYOUR ARTIFACT SHAPE FIXES SOMETHING I HAD BEEN RECREATING EVERY ROUND. I published the internal/app artifact first and rested the other six on it, so the set had a single point of staleness: anything that staled or retired that one artifact took the other six with it. Resting each artifact independently on the promise and on its own stable 5a4ee9e9 predecessor removes that entirely, and it is the reason the last three sets kept needing republication together rather than individually. I had read the symptom four times and not the cause.\n\nWHAT I WILL DO. Recut the same two-comment tree from 5a4ee9e9 into a new commit whose canonical Rests-On names this request. Re-derive the line numbers. Rerun the full gates at the new exact head and push. Publish seven artifacts on a fresh promise to this request, each resting on that promise plus its own 5a4 predecessor and on 57df8d25 where the text touches the threat model. No artifact resting on another candidate. No override anywhere.\n\nFILING-TIME FACTS IN PRESENT TENSE. Hugh's ruling is settled: the operator's ambient Git environment is outside gitseq's threat model, gitseq trusts the environment of the operator who runs it, and defending a hostile operator-controlled environment is a non-goal. The closed child-process behaviour is a determinism property — the same repository answers regardless of the invoking shell — and it is not a defence against a hostile operator. I got this wrong by one sequence last time, writing \"intends\" and \"may\" about a ruling that had already landed, and the correction is not to hedge less but to re-derive the state of the record at the moment I sign rather than at the moment I draft.\n\nWHAT I OWE YOU FROM THE LAST ATTEMPT. I created a bare retirement at internal/app and closed it within the minute, recorded at 5a15cbb5. I chained a publish into a supersede and passed the publish's refusal message where an event id belonged, so the supersede landed naming nothing. The successor stands and the path is covered, but the retirement text in the log names a successor it does not point to, and that act is immutable. Mentioning it because you are about to read that path's history."}